---
title: S.M.A.R.T.
order: 5
description: Disk health monitoring with smartctl and smartd
tags: [disk, smart, smartctl, smartd, health]
---

S.M.A.R.T. (Self-Monitoring, Analysis, and Reporting Technology) monitors disk health and predicts failures. Requires the smartmontools package.

@code bash
# Install smartmontools
sudo pacman -S smartmontools

@text
Querying disk health with smartctl.

@code bash
# Check if SMART is available and enabled
sudo smartctl -i /dev/sda

# Enable SMART on a drive
sudo smartctl -s on /dev/sda

# Quick health check (pass/fail)
sudo smartctl -H /dev/sda

# Show all SMART attributes
sudo smartctl -A /dev/sda

# Full info: health + attributes + capabilities
sudo smartctl -a /dev/sda

# Show error log
sudo smartctl -l error /dev/sda

# Show self-test results
sudo smartctl -l selftest /dev/sda

@text
Key SMART attributes to watch (lower raw values are better for most):

  5   Reallocated_Sector_Ct   — bad sectors remapped (any > 0 is concerning)
  187 Reported_Uncorrect      — uncorrectable errors
  188 Command_Timeout         — aborted operations
  197 Current_Pending_Sector  — unstable sectors waiting for remap
  198 Offline_Uncorrectable   — sectors that couldn't be read offline
  199 UDMA_CRC_Error_Count    — cable/interface errors

For NVMe drives, check: Critical Warning, Media Errors, Percentage Used.

@text
Run SMART self-tests.

@code bash
# Short self-test (~2 minutes)
sudo smartctl -t short /dev/sda

# Long self-test (can take hours depending on disk size)
sudo smartctl -t long /dev/sda

# Conveyance test (post-shipping check, ~5 minutes)
sudo smartctl -t conveyance /dev/sda

# Check test progress
sudo smartctl -l selftest /dev/sda

# Abort a running test
sudo smartctl -X /dev/sda

@text
NVMe drives use a different info path.

@code bash
# NVMe health info
sudo smartctl -a /dev/nvme0

# Or specify the namespace
sudo smartctl -a /dev/nvme0n1

# NVMe-specific: show percentage used, power-on hours, media errors
sudo smartctl -A /dev/nvme0n1

@text
Configure smartd for continuous monitoring. On Artix with OpenRC, the service is managed via rc-service.

@code bash
# Edit smartd configuration
sudo vi /etc/smartd.conf

# Monitor all drives, check every 30 minutes, email on problems:
# DEVICESCAN -a -o on -S on -n standby,q -s (S/../.././02|L/../../6/03) -m root

# Monitor specific drive with temperature tracking
# /dev/sda -a -o on -S on -W 4,45,55 -m root

# Start/enable smartd service (OpenRC)
sudo rc-service smartd start
sudo rc-update add smartd default

# Check smartd status
sudo rc-service smartd status

@text
Quick disk health summary script.

@code bash
# One-liner: check health of all drives
for dev in /dev/sd? /dev/nvme?n?; do
  [ -e "$dev" ] && echo "=== $dev ===" && sudo smartctl -H "$dev" | grep -i "result\|status"
done
